-- db/seed-data.sql — SNAPSHOT of the processed factor set.
--
-- Generated from the live development database with:
--   docker exec calamity-db pg_dump -U calamity -d calamity --data-only --inserts \
--     --no-owner --strict-names \
--     --table=public.factors --table=public.citations \
--     --table=public.factor_revisions --table=public.projections \
--     --table=public.requirements --table=public.requirement_efforts \
--     --table=public.counter_effort_candidates \
--     --table=public.organisations --table=public.organisation_links
--
-- --strict-names is not decoration. The previous revision of this file still
-- named --table=public.counter_efforts, which migration 017 had already
-- replaced with organisations + organisation_links. Without --strict-names a
-- --table pattern that matches nothing is silently skipped, so the snapshot
-- dropped 150 organisations and 187 links while pg_dump exited 0. That is the
-- precise failure apply-seed-data.sql's verification query warns about: factors
-- load, the routing surface does not, and every factor in the UI reads "no
-- effort found addressing this" — a wrong finding rather than a blank space.
-- Keep the flag, and re-check this list whenever a migration renames a table.
--
-- Unlike db/seed.sql (generated from shared/seed.ts, guaranteeing parity with
-- seed mode), this is a capture of the INGESTED and PROCESSED set: everything
-- the pipeline found, the reputability gate admitted, and the backfills scored —
-- significance bands, closesWindow judgements, quantity thresholds, the
-- projections that date them, the contingency chains under each crossed
-- threshold, and the organisations those chains route to.
--
-- Placement (migration 018) is carried: location_kind distinguishes a point a
-- source measured from one we chose to stand for a global phenomenon, and
-- location_note records the reason for every representative pin. A snapshot
-- taken before 018 cannot be loaded into a database that has it — the
-- factors_location_kind_check constraint requires (lat IS NULL) =
-- (location_kind IS NULL), so every placed row without a kind is rejected.
--
-- APPLYING THIS REPLACES, IT DOES NOT MERGE. An earlier revision used
-- ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, which is right for a fresh database and useless for
-- an existing one: every row already present kept its old values, so a target
-- that already had the factors gained none of the re-scoring. Load it via
-- db/apply-seed-data.sql, which truncates the owned tables inside one
-- transaction first.
--
-- `requirements` self-references (parent_id), so pg_dump warns about a circular
-- FK and the rows are NOT in dependency order. This is safe here only because
-- apply-seed-data.sql sets session_replication_role = replica, which disables FK
-- checking for the load. Applying this file directly, without that, can fail on
-- a child row that precedes its parent.
--
-- counter_effort_candidates carries the REJECTED candidates too, deliberately.
-- They are what makes a future gate change replayable (`npm run replay:efforts`)
-- instead of a re-crawl, and dropping them from the snapshot would mean the
-- production database could only ever re-tighten, never re-open.
--
-- Embeddings ARE included. They are most of the file's size, but restoring
-- without them leaves dedupe silently degraded until a full re-embed, which
-- costs real money — a bigger file is the cheaper failure.
--
--
-- PostgreSQL database dump
--

