s in a coral‐dominated state, implying reversal requires CO₂ below this threshold. Source 3 reports coral recovery times vary from 30 years to more than a century, with restoration projects taking decades to over a century depending on disturbance severity.", "sourceUrl": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3597261/", "timescaleLowYears": 30}, "closesWindow": true, "quantityThreshold": {"unit": "°C", "value": 1.2, "baseline": "pre-industrial (1850-1900)", "lowValue": 0.7, "quantity": "global mean surface temperature", "highValue": 1.5, "projectionId": "d4f74477-e78a-42be-a2a9-6c3128928298"}}', 0.95, 'The source is NOAA''s official ocean service website (oceanservice.noaa.gov), a U.S. government scientific agency with high editorial standards and authority in ocean and climate science. The supporting quote directly matches the claim''s assertion that warming and acidification lower the threshold for coral–algal phase shifts under overfishing. Although the claim includes additional details about impaired growth and increased mortality, the quote captures the core relationship, and the source is sufficiently credible to establish the claim at a high level.', '{ocean,biosphere}', NULL, 'planetary', 'representative', 'Great Barrier Reef — The Great Barrier Reef is the world''s largest and most iconic coral reef system, where the combined effects of ocean warming and acidification are severely impairing coral growth and resilience.');
